Z||000000||batboy||03-02-2000||04:54 PM||||My 85 GT was ok yesterday, but today I noticed a little exhaust noise, especially before the engine fully warms up to operating temperature. Sounds like the dreaded cracked firewall side exhaust manifold. Curses! Why couldn't Pontiac design a decent manifold instead of this piece of guano that cracks if you just look at it cross-eyed. I'll probably buy the manifold and then have the local muffler shop replace it and deal with the rusted and/or broken bolts.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gokart||03-02-2000||05:03 PM||||Is there a better aftermarket manifold that you can replace it with? Be good to know when mine gives out. Thanks Bat!||||reg||5
Z||000002||ChadMan||03-02-2000||10:40 PM||||I have looked at several V6 Fieros lately, and about half of them have leaky or broken exhaust manifolds.<P>How hard is it to replace the firewall-side manifold?  Is it easier to reach it from the top or from the bottom?  And is it necessary to remove anything major (like the decklid, or the exhaust from the cat back) to replace the manifold? ||||reg||5
Z||000003||Carrolles||03-02-2000||11:12 PM||||Mine did the same thing.  When I pulled the engine to do a clutch job I found a couple of loose exhaust manifold bolts.  The manifolds aren't always cracked when you hear the tic-tic-tic.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||03-02-2000||11:19 PM||||I used to do that exhaust work myself when I was poor and dumb. That's why I have a good paying job, so I can farm out the work when I want<P>Usually, I hate anyone working on my babies, but this muffler shop I go to has done work on several of my cars now. Including replacing half the exhaust on my 87 SE about 2 years ago.<P>Twist just one of those exhaust manifold bolts off in the head and you'll understand what I'm saying. A little heat from a torch often helps loosen up stubborn bolts.<P>I'm looking for a good price on a new manifold. Hate to go to the dealer, they seem to have such a high mark-up. I'll let you good folks know if I find a bargain.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sdm1964||03-03-2000||06:03 AM||||Glad you asked I just did mine last week, First jack up rear of car,heat shield is held in , top left, right and center, you have to get this off to access the other bolts, HINT, drop down the air compressor and it's brackets to get to the bottom right bolt. I fought with this bolt for the longest,after dropping down the compressor it made plenty of room to get it in and out, I did not remove the deck, you have to go from the top and bottom.I destroyed my heat shield nuts did not want to come off.I used tons of anti-seize on the threads. I used a stainless steel rod when i welded up the cracks, as not to warp the manifold. Good luck and start early!!! ||||reg||13
